    "url": "/best-air-purifier-for-mold/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-02", modified: "2026-11-02" });
const title = "6 Best Whole House Fans in 2026: Ranked by Climate and Attic";
const description =
  "Six whole house fans compared on airflow, noise, damper insulation, and attic fit, plus how to size CFM, vent the attic, and run one safely with gas appliances.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "name": "QuietCool Stealth Pro 3.3",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "Ducted, Mid-Size Home",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "id": "quietcool-stealth-pro",
    "pros": [
      "Ducted design isolates motor noise in the attic",
      "Insulated, gasketed ceiling damper closes when the fan is off",
      "Airflow suited to many homes of roughly 1,500 to 2,000 square feet",
      "Two-speed operation for quiet overnight running",
      "Long manufacturer warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs adequate attic venting, often more than the house already has",
      "Flexible duct takes up attic space"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best balance of airflow, quiet running, and winter sealing for a typical single-story or small two-story home.",
    "description": "QuietCool pioneered the ducted approach that changed how whole house fans sound, and the Stealth Pro 3.3 is the model that fits the largest number of homes. The motor hangs in the attic at the end of a flexible insulated duct, a few feet away from the ceiling grille, so the vibration and hum that made older fans so loud stay mostly in the attic. The number in the name points to its airflow class, which suits many homes in the 1,500 to 2,000 square foot range on the common two-to-three CFM per square foot rule. The ceiling damper is insulated and closes when the fan stops, which limits the winter heat loss that made traditional louvered fans unpopular. Two speeds let you run high in the evening to flush heat, then drop to low overnight. Owners commonly mention how quiet it is compared with older fans and how quickly the house cools once the windows are open. The practical catch is attic venting: a fan this size needs several square feet of net free vent area, and many houses need extra vents added."
  },
  {
    "name": "QuietCool Classic 3.4",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "category": "Ducted, Value Line",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "id": "quietcool-classic",
    "pros": [
      "Same ducted, noise-isolating concept at a lower price",
      "Lighter, simpler unit that is easy to install",
      "Strong airflow for its price class",
      "Ceiling grille fits standard joist spacing",
      "Widely available at home centers"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Damper insulation is less robust than the Stealth line",
      "Single speed on some configurations"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A cost-effective way to get ducted quiet operation if you can live with a less heavily insulated damper.",
    "description": "The Classic line is QuietCool's entry point, and it delivers the main benefit of ducted design at a lower price: the motor sits in the attic, separated from the ceiling by an insulated duct, so it is far quieter in the living space than an old-style direct-mount fan. Airflow in the 3.4 class covers many medium-sized homes. Installation is relatively simple for a capable DIYer with attic access and electrical experience, since the unit hangs from rafters with straps and the grille fits between standard joists. Where it gives ground to the Stealth Pro is the damper. The Classic seals reasonably well but carries less insulation, so in a cold climate expect more winter heat loss through the grille unless you add an insulated cover. Some configurations are single-speed, which is less flexible for overnight running. Owners generally rate it highly for value and noise compared with traditional fans. If you live somewhere with mild winters and want to keep the budget for added attic vents, this is the sensible pick."
  },
  {
    "name": "Centric Air 3.5",
    "badge": "Best Efficiency",
    "category": "Ducted, High-Efficiency Motor",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "id": "centric-air-3-5",
    "pros": [
      "Electronically commutated motor designed for low power draw",
      "Variable speed control for fine-tuning airflow",
      "Insulated damper for reduced heat loss",
      "Ducted design for quiet operation",
      "Good airflow for mid- to large-size homes"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more than comparable QuietCool models",
      "Fewer retail outlets, so often bought through installers or online"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A strong pick for owners who will run the fan for long hours and want the lowest electricity use per cubic foot moved.",
    "description": "Centric Air competes directly with QuietCool on ducted whole house fans, and its main argument is efficiency. The maker builds its fans around electronically commutated motors, the type found in high-efficiency furnace blowers, which typically move more air per watt than conventional motors and allow smooth variable speed rather than one or two fixed settings. If you plan to run the fan for many hours a night through a long dry summer, that efficiency adds up. Variable speed also helps with noise: you can dial airflow down to the point where it is barely audible in a bedroom while still pulling cool air through the house. The 3.5 model sits in the mid-to-large range, suiting many homes of roughly 1,750 to 2,300 square feet. The damper is insulated and closes when the fan is off. The trade-offs are price, which tends to run above QuietCool for similar airflow, and distribution, since you are more likely to buy it online or through an installer than at a big-box store. Installers who carry both lines often describe them as comparable in quality."
  },
  {
    "name": "Tamarack HV1000 R50",
    "badge": "Best for Cold Climates",
    "category": "Between-Joist, Insulated Doors",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "id": "tamarack-hv1000",
    "pros": [
      "Motorized insulated doors rated around R-50 seal the opening when off",
      "Installs between ceiling joists with no duct or large opening",
      "Very quiet, smaller airflow per unit",
      "Multiple units can be zoned around the house",
      "Minimal winter heat loss"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Lower airflow per unit, so larger homes need two or more",
      "Higher cost per CFM than ducted fans"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right choice where winters are cold and air sealing matters, or where you want quiet zoned fans in several rooms.",
    "description": "Tamarack takes a different approach from the ducted fans above. The HV1000 is a compact unit that sits between ceiling joists, and when it shuts off, motorized doors with heavy insulation close over the opening. The R50 in the name refers to that insulation rating. For anyone in a cold climate, that matters a great deal, because a whole house fan opening is otherwise a hole in the ceiling for six months of the year. The trade-off is airflow. At roughly 1,000 CFM per unit, a single HV1000 suits a small home, a cabin, or a bedroom wing rather than a large house. Many owners install two or three in different areas, which has its own advantages: you can run only the units in occupied zones, and each one is very quiet. Installation is simpler than a large ducted fan, since there is no big duct to hang. Cost per CFM is higher than QuietCool or Centric Air, so in mild climates the ducted fans are better value. In places with real winters, the Tamarack is the most airtight option here."
  },
  {
    "name": "QuietCool Trident Pro 4.0",
    "badge": "Best for Large Homes",
    "category": "Ducted, Large Home",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "id": "quietcool-trident-pro",
    "pros": [
      "High airflow for large homes in a single unit",
      "Ducted design keeps noise in the attic",
      "Insulated damper",
      "Multi-speed control",
      "Fewer ceiling openings than running several smaller fans"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Demands a lot of attic vent area",
      "Overkill and more expensive than needed for smaller homes"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A single-unit solution for homes over roughly 2,300 square feet that have the attic venting to support it.",
    "description": "Large homes face a choice between several smaller fans and one big one, and the Trident Pro is QuietCool's case for one big one. Its airflow class suits homes well above 2,000 square feet on the usual sizing rule, delivered through the same ducted layout that keeps the motor noise in the attic. Multi-speed control lets you run it hard during the first hour of the evening and much more quietly through the night. The insulated damper limits winter heat loss. The real constraint is the attic. A fan moving this much air needs a correspondingly large net free vent area, and if the attic cannot exhaust that volume, pressure builds, airflow falls, and hot attic air can push back through ceiling fixtures and gaps. Before buying, calculate the vent area you need and confirm the attic has it or can be given it with gable, ridge, or soffit vents. For homes under about 2,000 square feet, a smaller fan does the same work for less money and less venting work."
  },
  {
    "name": "Master Flow 24 in. Whole House Fan",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "category": "Direct-Mount Traditional",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "id": "master-flow-whf",
