      "Low purchase price",
      "Moves a lot of air for the money",
      "Simple, proven direct-mount design",
      "Shutter louvers close automatically when the fan stops",
      "Easy to find replacement parts"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Much louder than ducted fans because the motor sits right above the ceiling",
      "Louvered shutter leaks heat in winter without an added cover"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth it only where budget rules and noise is acceptable, and only with an insulated winter cover.",
    "description": "Before ducted fans, the typical whole house fan followed this pattern: a large fan mounted directly over a ceiling opening, with a louvered shutter below that opens when the fan pushes air and falls closed when it stops. The Master Flow 24-inch model keeps that design and its main advantage, price. It costs a fraction of a ducted fan and moves a substantial amount of air, which is why it remains popular for garages, workshops, and budget installs. The drawbacks are well known. Because the motor sits right above the ceiling, it is much louder in the room below, and owners commonly describe it as too loud to sleep with on high. The louvers close by gravity and do not seal well, so in winter warm air leaks into the attic unless you add an insulated winter cover, fitted either in the attic or on the ceiling side. Installation also involves cutting a larger ceiling opening and framing it. If your summers are short and your budget is tight, it works. If you plan to run the fan nightly for months, a ducted fan is worth the difference."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Whole House Fan",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-whole-house-fan/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many CFM should my whole house fan move?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A common rule is two to three CFM per square foot of living area, so a 1,500 square foot home wants roughly 3,000 to 4,500 CFM. Hotter climates sit at the upper end. Check that the result gives 15 to 30 air changes per hour, and confirm the attic has enough net free vent area to exhaust that volume."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many windows do I need to open?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Open roughly twice as much window area as your attic net free vent area, spread through the house. For a typical mid-size fan, that often means several windows opened a few inches each, or a few opened fully. Opening windows in the rooms you want cooled most directs the airflow there. Always open windows before starting the fan."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Whole house fan or attic fan: what separates them?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. An attic fan only exhausts air from the attic and has a limited effect on living-space temperature. A whole house fan pulls air through open windows, through the living space, and out through the attic, which cools the rooms directly. Whole house fans also flush the attic, so they reduce next-day heat gain from above."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a whole house fan work where summers are humid?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only partially. In humid climates, nights are often too warm and sticky for the fan to help, and running it can bring moisture into the house. It can still be useful in spring and fall when evenings are cool and dry. In the Gulf Coast and Southeast, most homeowners get better value from other efficiency upgrades."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does a whole house fan cost to run?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most modern ducted fans draw roughly 100 to 600 watts depending on size and speed, compared with several thousand watts for central air conditioning. At average U.S. electricity prices, running a mid-size fan for a full night costs a few cents to a few tens of cents, which is why owners in suitable climates see substantial cooling savings."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a whole house fan be dangerous with gas appliances?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can if the house is depressurized. Running the fan with too few windows open can pull exhaust, including carbon monoxide, back down the vent of an atmospheric gas water heater, furnace, or fireplace. Always open plenty of windows first, keep CO alarms on every level, and have an installer review combustion venting before installation."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I install a whole house fan myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many ducted fans are designed for DIY installation by someone comfortable working in an attic and following detailed instructions. The electrical connection must meet local code, may require a dedicated circuit and permit, and is best done by a licensed electrician if you have not done similar work. Direct-mount fans that require cutting joists are a bigger job."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
