              <a href="#winter" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Winter Heat Loss</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">13.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The fan never chills anything. It replaces hot indoor air with cooler outdoor air, fast, by pulling it through open windows and pushing it out through the attic. When evenings drop into the 60s or low 70s and the air is dry, that can mean leaving the air conditioner off for much of the summer. When nights are warm and muggy, no fan will help, and you should spend the money elsewhere.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Whole House Fans, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rankings draw on manufacturer airflow and power ratings, damper and insulation design, warranty terms, installation requirements, and consistent themes in owner and installer feedback. We did not install or measure these fans; airflow figures below come from the makers and are rounded.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="how-it-cools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How a Whole House Fan Cools a House
          </h2>

          <p>
            Through a hot day, walls, floors, furniture, and the air itself absorb heat. By evening, even when the temperature outside has dropped into the 60s, the house can still sit in the high 70s or low 80s because all that mass keeps releasing warmth. A whole house fan pulls air in through open windows, sends it through the living space, and exhausts it into the attic, where it leaves through the attic vents. Moving large volumes, often 15 to 30 air changes an hour, it replaces indoor air with outdoor air in minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            That has two effects. First, the air temperature indoors drops toward the outdoor temperature quickly. Second, the steady flow of cooler air through the house gradually pulls stored heat out of the building itself, so the house starts the next morning cooler and takes longer to warm up. In dry climates with large day-to-night temperature swings, that can delay or eliminate air conditioning for most of the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            A whole house fan also flushes the attic. Attics often reach 130 degrees or more on summer afternoons, and that heat radiates down into the ceiling below. Pushing large volumes of cooler air through the attic every evening lowers its temperature, which reduces heat gain into the house the following day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not confuse it with an attic fan. An attic ventilator, usually mounted on the roof or gable, only moves air from the attic to the outside, and its effect on living-space temperature is small. The whole house version pushes air across the rooms people occupy, and that flow is what does the cooling. The two are sometimes confused at the store, so check the product description.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="climate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Your Climate Right for One?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The test is simple. On the evenings you would like to stop running the air conditioner, does the outdoor air drop below about 75 to 78 degrees, and does it feel dry? If yes, a whole house fan will probably pay for itself. If the evenings stay warm or sticky, it will not, and running it will drag humid air into the house that the air conditioner then has to deal with.
          </p>

          <p>
            The strongest fits are dry Western climates such as much of California, the Mountain West, and the interior Pacific Northwest, where summer nights often fall into the 50s and 60s. Many Midwestern and Northeastern areas also see good results in late spring, early summer, and early fall, even if peak summer nights are too humid. The Gulf Coast and the Southeast are generally poor fits outside of the shoulder seasons.
          </p>

