          <p>
            Air quality is the other consideration. During wildfire smoke events or high pollen counts, pulling large volumes of outdoor air through the house is the opposite of what you want. Plan to switch to air conditioning and filtration on those days. Allergy sufferers may also prefer to run the fan only in the late evening and overnight, when some pollens are lower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Humid basements deserve a mention. Whole house fans pull from the windows you open, so keeping basement windows closed and running a dehumidifier from our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> prevents the fan from drawing humid outdoor air down into the coolest part of the house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Ducted Versus Direct-Mount Fans
          </h2>

          <p>
            Direct-mount fans sit right above a ceiling opening, often in a hallway, with a louvered shutter below. They move a lot of air for the money, but because the motor and blades are directly over the room, they are loud, and the louvers leak heat in winter. They also need a larger ceiling opening, typically cut across joists and reframed, which adds to installation work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ducted fans, such as the QuietCool and Centric Air models, put the motor several feet away in the attic and connect it to a ceiling grille with a flexible insulated duct. The duct absorbs much of the noise and vibration, and the grille usually fits between standard joists. Most include an insulated damper that closes when the fan stops. They cost more but are far quieter and better sealed, which is why most new installations today are ducted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between-joist units with insulated doors, such as the Tamarack HV1000, are a third type. They move less air per unit but seal tightly, so they work well in cold climates or as zoned fans in individual rooms. Larger homes typically use more than one.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
