          <p>
            For a home that will run the fan every night through summer, ducted is worth the extra cost. For a garage or workshop where noise does not matter and the fan runs only on the hottest days, a direct-mount fan is fine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sizing a Whole House Fan in CFM
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fan capacity is rated in cubic feet per minute. The usual sizing rule is two to three CFM per square foot of conditioned living area, with the lower end suited to cooler climates and the higher end to hotter ones. A 1,500 square foot home therefore wants roughly 3,000 to 4,500 CFM. Count only the floors the fan will draw through; an unfinished basement with closed windows does not count.
          </p>

          <p>
            Another way to check is air changes per hour. Multiply floor area by average ceiling height to get the house volume, then divide the fan's CFM by that volume and multiply by 60. A result between 15 and 30 air changes per hour is the useful range. Below 15, the fan feels weak; above 30, you need a lot of attic vent area and open window area to support it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Sizing at 2-3 CFM per Square Foot</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1,000 sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">2,000-3,000 CFM</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1,500 sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">3,000-4,500 CFM</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2,000 sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">4,000-6,000 CFM</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2,500 sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">5,000-7,500 CFM</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two-story homes benefit from placing the fan on the top floor, near the center, usually in a hallway ceiling, so it draws air up through the stairwell from windows on both floors. In a sprawling single-story house, two smaller fans at opposite ends often work better than one large fan in the middle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bigger is not always better. An oversized fan without enough attic venting pressurizes the attic, which can push hot air and dust back down through recessed lights and ceiling gaps, and in extreme cases interfere with gas appliance vents. Size to the house, then make sure the attic can exhaust what the fan moves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="venting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Attic Venting: The Part Most Installs Get Wrong
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every cubic foot the fan pushes into the attic has to leave through attic vents. Manufacturers commonly call for roughly 1 square foot of net free area per 750 CFM of fan capacity, and some recommend more. A 4,500 CFM fan therefore needs about 6 square feet of net free area, which is often double what a typical house already has.
          </p>

          <p>
            Net free area is not the size of the vent opening. Screens and louvers block a share of each vent, so a gable vent that measures 2 square feet across might provide only 1 square foot of net free area. The vent label or manufacturer data lists the net free figure. Ridge vents, soffit vents, gable vents, and roof louvers all count, as long as they are not blocked by insulation or debris.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the attic is short of vent area, add gable vents or roof louvers before the fan goes in. Signs of under-venting after installation include a whistling grille, doors that slam when the fan starts, dust puffing from recessed lights, and a weaker breeze than expected. Fixing venting after the fact is usually straightforward but easier to plan up front.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spray-foam insulated attics, where the foam is applied to the underside of the roof deck, are sealed by design and generally cannot accept a standard whole house fan without special venting arrangements. If your attic is foamed, talk to the fan maker or an installer before buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="operation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Running a Whole House Fan Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            Open windows before you switch the fan on, and keep them open the whole time it runs. A useful rule is to open about twice as much window area as the attic net free vent area, spread across the house. Opening windows in the rooms where you want the most cooling, such as bedrooms, steers the airflow there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start when the outdoor temperature falls below the indoor temperature, usually in early evening. Run on high for the first hour or two to flush the heat, then drop to low or a gentle setting overnight. Many owners use a timer switch so the fan shuts off in the early morning, before the outdoor air starts warming.
          </p>

          <p>
            Close up the house in the morning. Shut windows and draw shades on the sunny side once the outdoor temperature begins to rise. The goal is to trap the cool air and the cooled mass of the house inside for as long as possible. In dry climates, a house managed this way can stay comfortable well into the afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not run the fan and the air conditioner at the same time. The fan would push conditioned air into the attic while pulling warm outdoor air in. Switch from one to the other as conditions change. Ceiling fans, by contrast, pair well with a whole house fan because they add a breeze without bringing in outside air.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never run it with the windows closed</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A whole house fan with too little open window area depressurizes the house. That can pull exhaust gases, including carbon monoxide, down the flues of a gas water heater, furnace, or fireplace. Open windows first, every time, and keep working CO alarms on every level.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Installation and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installation involves cutting a ceiling opening, mounting the fan or hanging the ducted unit in the attic, and wiring it to a switch. Experienced DIYers often handle ducted models, which come with detailed instructions. Direct-mount fans that require cutting joists and reframing are a bigger carpentry job. Either way, follow the manufacturer's instructions exactly; they cover clearances, mounting, and electrical requirements.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electrical work must meet local code. Many fans need a dedicated circuit, and some jurisdictions require a permit. If you are not comfortable working in the panel and wiring a new circuit, hire a licensed electrician. Use the switch or timer the manufacturer specifies, and place it where children are less likely to start the fan with the windows closed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Combustion appliances need special attention. If your water heater, furnace, or boiler uses an atmospheric vent, the fan must never depressurize the house. Open windows generously, keep the fan away from the appliance area, and install carbon monoxide alarms. If you have a gas water heater, our guide to the <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a> explains the difference between atmospheric and power-vented models.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some fire codes require whole house fans to shut off automatically in a fire, often via a heat-sensing switch or a connection to the smoke alarm system, because a running fan can feed a fire with air. Check local requirements, and look for fans with built-in thermal cutoffs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Costs, Running Costs, and Savings
          </h2>

          <p>
            A ducted fan costs roughly $800 to $1,800 for the unit at the time of writing, with professional installation often adding $500 to $1,500 depending on attic access, venting work, and electrical requirements. Direct-mount fans are cheaper to buy but can cost as much to install if the ceiling opening needs reframing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running cost is where whole house fans shine. Most ducted fans draw roughly 100 to 600 watts depending on size and speed, compared with 2,000 to 5,000 watts for a typical central air conditioner. Running a mid-size fan for eight hours a night costs a few cents to a few tens of cents at average electricity rates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Savings depend entirely on climate and habits. In dry climates where the fan can replace evening and overnight air conditioning for most of the summer, owners commonly report substantial reductions in cooling bills. In humid climates, savings are smaller because the fan sees fewer useful hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Factor in venting upgrades and possible electrical work when budgeting. A fan that needs two new gable vents and a dedicated circuit can easily cost several hundred dollars more to install than the quote for the fan alone. Ask installers for a line-item quote.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Noise, Sleep, and Living With the Fan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Noise is the main reason people stop using a whole house fan, so it deserves as much attention as airflow. Two sounds matter: the hum and vibration of the motor, and the rush of air through the grille and open windows. Ducted designs reduce the first by putting distance and an insulated duct between the motor and the ceiling. The second depends on how hard the fan is pushing and how much open window area it has to draw from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Starving a fan of intake air makes it louder. When there is too little open window area, air accelerates through the gaps and whistles, and the grille can rattle. Opening a few more windows, even a couple of inches each, often quiets a fan more than any other change. The same applies to attic venting; an under-vented attic makes the fan work against back pressure, which raises the pitch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement matters for sleep. A grille in the hallway outside the bedrooms is common, and on high speed that hallway will be noisy. Owners who sleep with the fan on usually run it on high for the first hour or two after sunset, then switch to low before bed. Variable-speed fans, such as the Centric Air, make it easier to find a setting that is both effective and quiet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about the breeze as well as the sound. Airflow concentrates at the windows nearest the fan, so rooms far from it get less. Opening windows in distant bedrooms and partly closing the ones closest to the grille spreads the cooling more evenly through the house, and lets you run a lower, quieter speed for the same overall effect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="winter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Winter Heat Loss and How to Stop It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Any opening in a ceiling is a potential heat leak. Older direct-mount fans with gravity louvers are the worst offenders, because the louvers seal poorly and warm air rises straight into the attic all winter. In cold climates, that can add noticeably to heating bills and can carry moisture into the attic, where it condenses on cold roof sheathing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Insulated dampers on modern ducted fans address most of this, and between-joist units with heavily insulated doors address nearly all of it. If you have an older louvered fan, fit an insulated cover over the opening for winter, either a rigid foam box from the attic side or a magnetic or clip-on cover from below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seal around the fan housing and grille as well. A gap between the grille frame and the ceiling drywall can leak as much as the fan opening. Caulk or foam those gaps from the attic side, keeping required clearances around the motor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Attic moisture is worth a periodic look. Frost on the underside of the roof deck in winter, or dark staining on the sheathing near the fan, suggests warm, moist air is escaping into the attic. Better sealing usually fixes it; if the staining looks like mold, our roundup of the <a href="/best-mold-remover/">best mold removers</a> covers treatment for attic framing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Undersizing the attic vents</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most attics do not have enough net free vent area for a whole house fan, and adding the fan without more venting leaves it starved. Airflow drops, the attic pressurizes, and hot air can push back through ceiling fixtures. Calculate roughly 1 square foot of net free area per 750 CFM before installation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting the fan before opening windows</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running the fan against closed windows depressurizes the house and can pull flue gases from atmospheric gas appliances back into the living space. It also strains the motor. Make opening windows the first step every time, and consider a timer or interlock so the fan cannot run long unnoticed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it on humid nights</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A whole house fan brings outdoor air in as it is. On sticky nights that means humid air soaking into furniture, carpet, and drywall, which the air conditioner then has to remove the next day. Use the fan when the air is both cooler and reasonably dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for the biggest number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An oversized fan needs more attic vent area and more open windows than many homes can provide. It is also louder at any given cooling level than a right-sized fan running on a faster speed. Size at two to three CFM per square foot of living area.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring winter sealing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old louvered fans leak heated air into the attic all winter, adding to heating bills and carrying moisture into the roof space. Choose a fan with an insulated damper or doors, seal around the grille, and add an insulated cover to older fans before cold weather arrives.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many CFM should my whole house fan move?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A common rule is two to three CFM per square foot of living area, so a 1,500 square foot home wants roughly 3,000 to 4,500 CFM. Hotter climates sit at the upper end. Check that the result gives 15 to 30 air changes per hour, and confirm the attic has enough net free vent area to exhaust that volume.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many windows do I need to open?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Open roughly twice as much window area as your attic net free vent area, spread through the house. For a typical mid-size fan, that often means several windows opened a few inches each, or a few opened fully. Opening windows in the rooms you want cooled most directs the airflow there. Always open windows before starting the fan.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Whole house fan or attic fan: what separates them?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. An attic fan only exhausts air from the attic and has a limited effect on living-space temperature. A whole house fan pulls air through open windows, through the living space, and out through the attic, which cools the rooms directly. Whole house fans also flush the attic, so they reduce next-day heat gain from above.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a whole house fan work where summers are humid?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only partially. In humid climates, nights are often too warm and sticky for the fan to help, and running it can bring moisture into the house. It can still be useful in spring and fall when evenings are cool and dry. In the Gulf Coast and Southeast, most homeowners get better value from other efficiency upgrades.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does a whole house fan cost to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most modern ducted fans draw roughly 100 to 600 watts depending on size and speed, compared with several thousand watts for central air conditioning. At average U.S. electricity prices, running a mid-size fan for a full night costs a few cents to a few tens of cents, which is why owners in suitable climates see substantial cooling savings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a whole house fan be dangerous with gas appliances?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can if the house is depressurized. Running the fan with too few windows open can pull exhaust, including carbon monoxide, back down the vent of an atmospheric gas water heater, furnace, or fireplace. Always open plenty of windows first, keep CO alarms on every level, and have an installer review combustion venting before installation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a whole house fan myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many ducted fans are designed for DIY installation by someone comfortable working in an attic and following detailed instructions. The electrical connection must meet local code, may require a dedicated circuit and permit, and is best done by a licensed electrician if you have not done similar work. Direct-mount fans that require cutting joists are a bigger job.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

